"Fast memory is more expensive than slow storage.": "4390e090a221",
"In Lesson 5 you met two parts that both keep things: memory and storage. That seems wasteful. Why not build one kind of memory that is fast, holds everything, and never forgets? Engineers would love to. The trouble is that no single kind of memory can do all of that. The fast kinds are small and expensive. The large, cheap kinds are slow. So a computer uses two, and lets each do what it is good at.": "b2648758d11f",
"In Lesson 5 you met two parts that both keep things: memory and storage. That seems wasteful. Why not build one kind of memory that is fast, holds everything, and never forgets? Engineers would love to. The trouble is that no single kind of memory can do all of that. The fast kinds are small and expensive. The large, cheap kinds are slow. So a computer uses two, and lets each do what it is good at. Think of a desk and a bookcase. The bookcase holds hundreds of books, but to use one you have to get up and fetch it. The desk holds only a few things, but they are right in front of you. Storage is the bookcase. Memory is the desk. In Lesson 6 you saw that the processor fetches from memory again and again without stopping, so memory has to be the fast one. When you open a program, the computer copies it from storage into memory. The first time is the slow part, like walking to the bookcase. After that, the processor works with the copy in memory, which is quick. The original stays in storage. When you change a file, you are changing only the copy in memory. Saving means copying the new version back to storage. Memory has one more weakness: it needs power to hold on to anything. Switch the computer off, and everything in memory is gone. Storage does not lose its contents when the power is turned off. That is why work you have not saved can disappear, and why a computer needs a little time to start up: it has to copy what it needs from storage into memory all over again. What if you open more than memory can hold? The computer does not simply give up. It takes something you are not using at the moment and moves it out of memory onto storage, to make room. When you go back to it, the computer has to move it in again. Everything still works, but storage is slower, so the computer feels slow. That is the usual reason a computer with too many things open drags. So the two kinds of memory are a team. Storage is large and lasting. Memory is fast and close to the processor. A typical computer has many times more storage than memory, because storage is the cheaper of the two. In fact a computer has more than two levels: from tiny, very fast memory inside the processor itself, to memory, to storage, each one larger and slower than the one before it.": "32464dc582cb",
"Memory has one more weakness: it needs power to hold on to anything. Switch the computer off, and everything in memory is gone. Storage does not lose its contents when the power is turned off. That is why work you have not saved can disappear, and why a computer needs a little time to start up: it has to copy what it needs from storage into memory all over again.": "1c75070c5eb0",
